== Christ’s Union with His Beloved Body

=== §. 68. #emph[Of Christ’s forbearing to hate the church.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:29.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Just as the Lord does the church.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[This] confirmation of the pattern of a man’s self by a like pattern of the Lord relates to both parts of the showing of a man’s love to himself. It relates to the negative, and so it shows that the Lord does not hate his church.

And it relates to the affirmative, and so it shows that the Lord nourishes and cherishes his church.

The difference made between Esau, a type of the world (“Esau I have hated”),#footnote[Mal 1:2, 3.] and Jacob, a type of the church (“Jacob I have loved”), shows that the Lord is far from hating his church. The world, not the church, is the object of God’s hatred.

#emph[Objection.] The church herself#footnote[Deut 1:27.] and the enemies#footnote[Deut 9:28.] of the church often conclude from Christ’s dealings with her that he hates her.

#emph[Answer.] It is the flesh remaining in those who are of the church that makes them think so, not the spirit; and in the enemies of the church the flesh reigns altogether. But the things of God, and his mind and feelings, neither can nor may be judged by carnal eyes, eyes of flesh. The Spirit of God#footnote[Heb 12:6.] counts as evidences of God’s love those things which the flesh judges to be tokens of hatred; namely, corrections.

It is not because there is no matter for hatred in the church#footnote[Reason.] that Christ does not hate it. For #emph[by nature] all are of one and the same cursed stock, “children of wrath”;#footnote[Eph 2:3.] and even after our sanctification has begun, the flesh remaining in us, we daily give much occasion for hatred, if Christ should take the advantage against us that he might. Rather, it is the near union which Christ has made between himself and the church that keeps him from hating her. He has made her his spouse, and he will not hate his spouse. All the occasion of hatred that she gives, he will either wipe away or cover.

Wonderful is the comfort which every true member of the catholic church (the universal church)#footnote[1\. #emph[Use.] Comfort.] may reap from this. For so long as the wrath and hatred of the Lord is turned from us, nothing can make us miserable. In this respect we may rejoice not only in prosperity, but also in every kind of affliction. No calamity can move Christ to hate his church, but rather to pity it the more, as we do our own bodies. Indeed, though he is provoked by sin and sees it needful to correct his church, yet he will correct it in love, not in hatred; in mercy, not in wrath.

What now if all the world hates us? Seeing that Christ does not hate#footnote[2\. #emph[Use.] Encouragement.] us, we need not fear or be anxious. The subject who is sure of his king’s favor pays little regard to the hatred of others. This therefore is to be thought on, both to comfort us under the cross and to encourage us against the hatred of the world. So that none may pervert this comforting doctrine, let me add two cautions.

1\. That men do not deceive themselves with a bare name, thinking themselves to be #emph[of] the church when they are only #emph[in] it.#footnote[Jer 12:8.] Such Christ may hate.

2\. That those who are of the church do not grow insolent and provoke Christ too much to anger. For though he does not hate such, yet in wisdom he may correct them as severely as if he hated them, and make them repent their folly and insolence again and again.

=== §. 69. #emph[Of Christ’s nourishing and cherishing his church.]

2\. That #emph[the Lord nourishes and cherishes his church] is evident from his continual providence over her in all ages. When he first created man, he#footnote[Gen 1:28, 29.] provided beforehand all things needful to nourish and cherish him. When he was moved to destroy the earth and all living things on it, he took care of his church: he provided an ark to keep her out of#footnote[Gen 6:14, 21.] the waters, and stored up in the ark all things needful for her. When he purposed to bring a famine on the world, he sent a man beforehand to lay up provision for his church. When#footnote[Gen 45:7. Exod 16:15; 17:6.] his church was in a barren and dry wilderness, he gave them bread from heaven and water out of the rock, and kept their clothing from wearing out and their feet from swelling.#footnote[Deut 8:4.] After this he brought his church into a land flowing with milk and honey, and so long as it remained faithful he preserved it in that pleasant and plentiful land. So he dealt with the church in her childhood; and so also has he dealt with her in her riper age under the gospel, as the experience of all ages may witness. Nor has he nourished and cherished her only with temporal blessings, but also with all needful spiritual blessings. His word and sacraments, his Spirit and the graces of the Spirit, he has given her in all ages for that purpose. Yes, he has fed her with his own flesh and blood,#footnote[John 6:55.] and clothed her with his own righteousness. #footnote[Isa 61:10.]

Let us learn from whom we receive all needful things, both#footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] spiritual and temporal, for soul and body, so that accordingly#footnote[Ascribe all to Christ.] we may give him the praise for all. And let us not be like the ungrateful Israelites, who#footnote[Ezek 20:11, 12.] disregarded the means of spiritual#footnote[Hos 8:12.] nourishment, and#footnote[Jer 44:17. Hos 2:5, 8.] ascribed the means of their temporal nourishing and cherishing to their idols. In this respect the prophet makes them#footnote[Isa 1:3.] worse than the ox and the donkey, two of the most brutish beasts there are. Oh, let us take heed that the like is not cast up against us. The Lord has nourished and cherished us in this land not sparingly but most liberally and bountifully, and that with both temporal and spiritual blessings, so that he may justly say, “What more could I have done#footnote[Isa 5:4.] for My vineyard than I already did for it?”

Let us also learn to depend on Christ for all things that we#footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] lack. We need not fear poverty. Though we do not have the#footnote[Depend on Christ for all things.] plenty we could wish, yet we will have sufficiency. Christ will not allow his church to famish for lack of food, nor freeze for lack of clothing, whether temporal for the body or spiritual for the soul. He who can and will perform it has said, “Never will I leave you, never will I forsake you.”#footnote[Heb 13:5.] Lazarus was not forsaken; witness the angels that carried his soul into Abraham’s#footnote[Luke 16:22.] bosom. If any of Christ’s church do perish for lack of outward means, it is because Christ by that means will advance them to that place where they will need nothing; so he does not forsake them.

=== §. 70. #emph[Of the union between Christ and the saints.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:30.]

    #text(style: "italic")[For we are members of His body, of His flesh, and of His bones.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] reason for the love of Christ already named, and for its fruits#footnote[Inference.] toward his church, is laid down here, as both the causal particle (FOR) and the #emph[inference] of this verse from the former show. This reason is the near union between Christ and his church, set forth by a metaphor of the members of our body. By this he implies that, though there were no other reason to move a husband to love his wife than the near union between them (they being#footnote[#emph[v.] 23.] one body,#footnote[#emph[v.] 31.] one flesh,#footnote[#emph[v.] 28.] one self), that would be enough; for by that alone is Christ moved to love his church.

The mystery of our spiritual union with Christ is laid down here, and as fully and distinctly (though very briefly)#footnote[Sum,] as in any place of Scripture. I will endeavor to open it as plainly as I can.

#emph[We are.] Here the apostle changes both person and number.#footnote[ἐσμέν. Paul ranks himself among the members of Christ.] For before, he spoke of the church as of another, in the third person, and as of one, in the singular number. But here he speaks of the same church in the first person, including himself, and in the plural number, including all others like himself #emph[(the elect of God, and saints by calling).] By this he gives us to understand what he means by the #emph[church,] namely #emph[the company of saints.] To this company he associates and joins himself, though he was a preacher of the gospel, an extraordinary preacher, an apostle. By this he shows that he was made partaker of the same grace, and saved by the same means, as others were. Well might he not grudge to rank himself in this privilege, because #emph[it is the highest degree of honor that can be]#footnote[Observation.] #emph[to be a member of the body of Christ:] much more than to be a preacher, a prophet, an apostle, or of any other eminent calling.

The metaphor used here #emph[(members of his body)] sets#footnote[Metaphors which set forth our union with Christ.] forth the near union between Christ and the saints. Many other metaphors are used in Scripture for the same purpose, such as #emph[foundation]#footnote[1 Pet 2:4, 5, 6.] and #emph[building, vine]#footnote[John 15:5.] and #emph[branches, husband]#footnote[2 Cor 11:2.] and #emph[wife,] and the like. All of them are very fit, but none is more proper and pertinent to the point than this of a #emph[body,] with its #emph[head] and #emph[members.] What nearer union can there be than between the head and members of the same body?

If the apostle had stopped here, we might have thought that he meant nothing other than what he meant#footnote[Eph 1:23; 4:16; 5:23.] before, where he called #emph[Christ a head,] and the #emph[church a body.] But in adding #emph[(of his flesh and of his bones)] he declares a further mystery still.

In general there is a difference between this phrase#footnote[τοῦ σώματος.] (OF #emph[his body)] and these (OF #emph[his flesh;]#footnote[ἐκ τῆς σαρκός. ἐκ τῶν ὀστέων.] and OF #emph[his bones).] The former is a sign of the#footnote[Corporis. \[Of the body.\]] genitive case; the two latter are a#footnote[Ex. \[Out of.\]] preposition. For distinction’s sake the two latter might have been translated #emph[out of his flesh, out of his bones,] or #emph[from his flesh, from his bones] (for a like phrase is so translated#footnote[Eph 4:16 ἐξ οὗ.] before, #emph[from whom]). But seeing these particles #emph[out of] or #emph[from] are ambiguous, the former translation may stand as the best, provided a difference is made in the sense, though there is none in the words.

The former #emph[(members of his body)] declares the union itself.

The latter #emph[(of his flesh and of his bones)] declares the #emph[means]#footnote[How we are of Christ’s flesh.] of making that union. This latter relates to what Adam said of Eve, “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh” (Gen 2:23). That is clear from the next verse, which the apostle takes out of the same place. It implies, then, that as Eve was made a woman out of Adam’s flesh and bones, so the church is made a church out of Christ’s flesh and bones.

1\. #emph[Question.] Was the very substance of the saints, their flesh and bones, taken out of Christ, as the substance of Eve was taken out of Adam?

#emph[Answer.] Not so, if the words are taken literally. For in that sense Christ may rather be said to be #emph[of our flesh] and #emph[of our bones,] because he took our nature, and that from a daughter of Adam. In this respect he is said to be “of the seed of David,”#footnote[2 Tim 2:8 ἐκ σπέρματος Δαβίδ.] and#footnote[Rom 9:5 ἐξ ὧν ὁ Χριστὸς τὸ κατὰ σάρκα.] of the Jews, “according to the flesh.” Besides, the apostle expressly says (v. 32), “This is a great mystery.” The mystery therefore must be searched out. To this end Christ must be considered as another Adam (and so the Holy Spirit calls him “the last Adam,” “the second man”),#footnote[1 Cor 15:45, 47.] that is, a #emph[stock,] a #emph[root] that gives a #emph[being] to branches sprouting out of him.

2\. #emph[Question.] What #emph[being] is it that we receive from Christ?#footnote[What #emph[being] we receive from Christ.]

#emph[Answer.] Not our #emph[natural being] (that we have from the parents of our flesh), but a #emph[supernatural] and #emph[spiritual being,] which the Scripture calls #emph[a new birth,]#footnote[Titus 3:5.] #emph[a new man,]#footnote[Eph 4:24.] #emph[a new creature.]#footnote[2 Cor 5:17.] This #emph[spiritual being] is not in regard of the substance of our soul or body, or of any of their powers or parts, faculties or members. For all these we have by lineal descent from Adam, and all sorts of men have all these, those who are not of the church as well as those who are of it. It is in regard of the integrity, goodness, and divine qualities which are in them,#footnote[2 Pet 1:4. Eph 4:24.] even that holiness and righteousness with which the church is endowed and adorned. As we are #emph[natural] men we are #emph[of Adam;] as we are #emph[spiritual] men we are #emph[of Christ.]

3\. #emph[Question.] Why is mention made of #emph[flesh] and #emph[bones]#footnote[Why we are said to be of Christ’s flesh. Gen 2:23.] in this spiritual being?

#emph[Answer.] 1. In allusion to the creation of Eve, so that by comparing this with that, #emph[this] might be the better understood.

2\. In regard of the Lord’s Supper, where the #emph[flesh] of Christ is mystically set before us to be spiritual food for us. So that, as before (v. 26) he showed the mystery of one sacrament, #emph[baptism,] here he might show the mystery of the other sacrament, #emph[the Lord’s Supper.]

3\. In relation to Christ’s human nature, by virtue of which we come to be united to Christ. For the divine nature of Christ is infinite, incomprehensible, incommunicable, and#footnote[#emph[Diuinitas vnigeniti facta est particeps mortalitatis nostrae, vt & nos participes eius immortalitatis essemus. Aug. Epist.] 57.] there is no proportion at all between it and us, so that we could not be united to it immediately. But Christ, by taking his human nature into the unity of his divine nature, made himself one with us, and us one with him; so that by his partaking of our mortality, we are made partakers of his immortality.

4\. #emph[Question.] Are we then united only to his human nature?

#emph[Answer.] No: we are united to his person, God-man. For as#footnote[We are united to Christ’s person.] the divine nature, in and by itself, is incommunicable, so the human nature, considered singly, in and by itself, is unprofitable. The Deity is the fountain of all life and grace; the flesh does not give life. But that spiritual life which flows originally and primarily from the Deity, as from a fountain, is conveyed into us by the humanity of Christ, as by a conduit.

5\. #emph[Question.] How can we who are on earth be united to his#footnote[How saints on earth are united to Christ in heaven.] human nature, which is contained in the highest heaven?

#emph[Answer.] This union, being supernatural and spiritual, needs no local presence for the making of it. That eternal Spirit which is in Christ is conveyed into each of the saints (as the soul of a man is into every member and part of his body), and by virtue of that Spirit they are all made one with Christ, and with one another: “by one Spirit we were all baptized into one body,”#footnote[1 Cor 12:12, 13.] which body is #emph[Christ.]

This is to be noted against these two errors. The first is#footnote[I. Error.] this: #emph[We are united first to the divine nature of Christ, which is everywhere, and by virtue of it to his human nature.]

#emph[Answer.] 1. The Deity (as we showed) cannot be communicated to us immediately, so this cannot be.

2\. Our union with Christ is spiritual, not physical or natural, so this local presence is not needed.

The second error is this:

#emph[The human nature of Christ has all the divine properties in it,]#footnote[II. Error.] so that it is present everywhere, and by reason of this we are united to Christ.

#emph[Answer.] This also is impossible and needless. The properties of a true body cannot possibly admit the incommunicable properties of the Deity. That implies a direct contradiction, namely that the finite should be infinite. It is needless also, because the union we speak of is (as we said) spiritual.

6\. #emph[Question.] What kind of union is this spiritual union?#footnote[Our union with Christ a true and real union.]

#emph[Answer.] A true, real union of our persons (bodies and souls) with the person of Christ (God and man). For as the Holy Spirit united the divine and human natures of Christ in the virgin’s womb, and made them one person, by reason of which Christ is #emph[of our flesh and of our bones,] so the Spirit unites that person of Christ with our persons, by reason of which we are #emph[of his flesh, and of his bones.] There is a great difference between the kinds of these unions. The union of Christ’s two natures is hypostatical and essential: they make one person. But the union of Christ’s person and ours is spiritual and mystical: they make one mystical body. Yet there is no difference in the reality and truth of these unions. Our union with Christ is not one bit less real and true because it is mystical and spiritual. Those who have the same Spirit are as truly one as those parts which have the same soul. The effects which proceed from this union show its truth. For the Spirit that sanctified Christ in his mother’s womb sanctifies us also; the Spirit that gave him life gives#footnote[Rom 8:11. Eph 2:6.] us life; the Spirit that raised him from death raises us; the Spirit that exalted him exalts us. The many resemblances which the Scripture uses to set forth this union show its truth. But it is set forth most vividly by the resemblance Christ makes between it and his union with his Father. “I pray” (he says of all his saints) “that all of them#footnote[John 17:21, 22.] may be one, as You, Father, are in Me, and I am in You. May they also be in Us … that they may be one as We are one.” This note of comparison #emph[(as)] is to be taken not of the #emph[kind,] but of the #emph[truth] of these unions: our union with Christ is as true as Christ’s union with his Father.

So true is this union that not only Jesus himself, but all the#footnote[#emph[Unusquisque Christianus non incongruè sustinet personam Christi.] \[Every Christian not unfittingly bears the person of Christ.\] #emph[Aug. de Gen. l.] 2. #emph[c.] 24. #emph[vide eundem quaest.] 69. \[See the same author, question 69.\]] saints who are members of this body, together with Jesus its head, are called CHRIST, 1 Cor 12:12. Gal 3:16.

This is to be noted against the fancy of those who imagine this union to be only in the imagination and fancy; or else only in agreement of spirit, heart, and will; or at most, in a sharing of spiritual graces.

7\. #emph[Question.] What is the bond by which this union is made,#footnote[With what bonds we are united to Christ. 1 John 4:13.] namely, by which Christ and the saints are made one?

#emph[Answer.] There is a double bond. One is on Christ’s part, namely the Spirit of Christ (for “by this we know that we dwell in Him, and He in us: He has given us of His Spirit”). The other is on the saints’ part, namely faith (for “Christ dwells in our hearts through faith”).#footnote[Eph 3:17.] The Spirit is conveyed into us when we are dead in sins, wholly flesh; but being in us, the Spirit breeds this blessed instrument of faith, by which we lay hold on Christ and grow into him as the graft into the stock. So, Christ laying hold on us by his Spirit, and we on him by faith, we come to be incorporated into him and made one body, as the graft and the stock are one tree.

8\. #emph[Question.] To what end has Christ so truly and nearly#footnote[Why Christ made the saints one with himself.] united us to himself?

#emph[Answer.] Not for any benefit to himself, but purely for the honor and good of the church. By this union the honor of Christ is communicated to the church, as the honor of a husband to his wife, and of a head to the body. Great also is the benefit which the church reaps by it. For by this means Christ is made more fit to do good to the church, as a head to the body; and the church is made more capable of receiving good from Christ, as a body from the head, being knit to it by the soul, and by veins, sinews, nerves, arteries, and other such ligaments.

Having thus laid open this great mystery by questions and answers as plainly as I can, I will further point out some of the excellent privileges which belong to the saints by virtue of it, and also some of the principal duties to which the saints are bound in regard of it.

=== §. 71. #emph[Of the privileges belonging to the saints even in this life because of their union with Christ.]

The privileges of the saints which arise from their union with Christ concern this life, the time of death, and the life to come.

In this life, these:

1\. #emph[A most glorious condition,] which is to be a part of Christ, a#footnote[1\. Saints are members of a glorious body.] member of his body. All the glory of Adam in paradise, or of the angels in heaven, is not comparable to this. In this respect the saints are said to be “crowned with glory and honor,” and to have all things put under their feet. Compare Ps 8:4, 5, #emph[etc.] with Hebrews 2:6, 7, #emph[etc.,] and you#super[+] will find the apostle apply to Christ what the prophet spoke indefinitely of man. Now those two places cannot be better reconciled than by#footnote[#emph[Coelo, & Angelis, & omni creatura honorabilior est ecclesia. Chrys. de Pentecost. serm.] 26.] this union of Christ and the saints. For seeing both make one body, which is #emph[Christ,] what is spoken of the body may be applied to the head, and what is spoken of the head may be applied to the body; for the same honor belongs to both. In this respect the church is more honorable than heaven, the angels, and every other creature.

2\. #emph[The attendance of good angels,] who are “sent to serve#footnote[2\. Saints attended by angels.] those who will be heirs of salvation,”#footnote[Heb 1:14. 2 Kgs 6:17.] because those heirs are of the body of Christ, who is their Lord. These are the “horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha”; and they are also round about every one of God’s saints in all their distresses, though we see them no more than the servant of the man of God saw them, till the Lord opened his eyes. That #emph[charge] which is given to the angels over the Son of God, “to guard him in all his ways,” and “to lift him up in their hands, so that he#footnote[Ps 91:11, 12.] will not strike his foot against a stone,” relates to this body which is #emph[Christ.]

3\. #emph[An honor to make Christ himself complete:] for as the individual#footnote[3\. Saints make Christ complete.] members make a natural body complete, so the individual saints make complete this body which is #emph[Christ.] In this respect the church is said to be “the fullness of Him who fills all in all.”#footnote[Eph 1:23.] Christ fills all things, and yet the church makes him full. This is to be understood of that voluntary condition to which Christ subjected himself, to be the head of a body; so that without the parts of the body he is incomplete, as a natural body is maimed and incomplete if it lacks even the least of its members. How can we now think anything but that he will preserve and keep safe all his saints? Will he restore to us all the parts of our natural body at the general resurrection, and will he lose any of the parts of his own mystical body?

4\. #emph[A kind of possession of heaven] while we are on earth. For#footnote[4\. Saints assured of salvation, and how.] what the head has possession of, the body and its individual members also have possession of. In this respect it is said, “God raised us up with Christ and seated us with Him in the#footnote[Eph 2:6.] heavenly realms.” And, “whoever … believes Him who sent Me has eternal#footnote[John 5:24.] life … he has crossed over from death to life.” And, “Whoever has the Son#footnote[1 John 5:12.] has life.” This is something more than hope; and it serves greatly to strengthen our hope, and to give us assurance of that heavenly inheritance.

They know neither the power of God nor the virtue of this union who deny that the saints have assurance of salvation. For (to follow this metaphor a little) suppose a man were cast into a river, and his head were able to lift and keep itself above water: would we not say that the man is safe enough, he is above water? This is the case of this mystical body. It is cast into the sea of this world, but Christ its head has lifted himself up and keeps himself aloft, even in heaven. Is there now any fear, any possibility, of the drowning of this body, or of any member of it? If any should be drowned, then either Christ must be drowned, or else that member must be pulled from Christ; both of which are impossible. So by virtue of this union we see how our safety depends on Christ’s safety: if he is safe, so are we; if we perish, so must he.

In this respect, “you#super[+] may be secure, O flesh and blood: you have#footnote[Securi estote caro & sanguis: vsurpastis coelum in Christo: negent & in coelo Christum qui vobis coelum negant. Tertul. de Resur.] gained heaven in Christ. Those who deny heaven to you#super[+] may also deny that Christ is in heaven.”

Learn here how to think of the resurrection, ascension, and safety of Christ: as of the resurrection, ascension, and safety of a head, in and with whom his body and all his members are raised, exalted, and preserved.

5\. #emph[A most happy kind of government] under which the saints are: even such as the members of a head are under. A head rules the body not as a cruel lord and tyrant, harshly,#footnote[5\. Difference between Christ governing the saints and the world.] inhumanly, basely, and slavishly, but meekly, gently, with great compassion and fellow feeling. Even so does Christ rule his church, binding up what is broken, healing what is maimed, directing what wanders, and stirring up what is dull. This privilege is all the greater because it belongs to the church alone. Though he has a golden scepter of grace and favor to hold out to his church (as Ahasuerus held out his to Esther),#footnote[Esth 5:2.] yet he has also “an iron scepter” to “break” the men of this world, and to “shatter them#footnote[Ps 2:9.] like pottery.” Though he has gone to prepare a place for his saints, so that where he is they may be also,#footnote[John 14:3.] yet he will “make his enemies his footstool.”#footnote[Ps 110:1.]

6\. #emph[An assurance of sufficient supply of all needful things] which#footnote[6\. Assurance of all needful supply.] the saints lack, and of safe protection from all things hurtful. For by reason of this union, Christ our head has a sense of our want and of our pain. On this ground he said to those who fed and visited his members, “You#super[+] gave Me something to eat … you visited Me”;#footnote[Matt 25:35. Acts 9:4.] and again, to Saul, who persecuted his members, “Saul, Saul, why do you persecute Me?”

#emph[Objection.] How is it, then, that the saints lack many things,#footnote[Why Christ allows the saints to lack, and to suffer.] and often suffer much pain and hurt?

#emph[Answer.] Christ in his wisdom sees it needful that they should lack and feel pain (and of this we are to be persuaded), or else he would not allow them to lack or to feel what they do. Therefore in all need, in every distress and danger, let us lift up our head to this #emph[our head.]

7\. #emph[A right to all that Adam lost.] For Christ is the “heir of all things”#footnote[7\. Saints have a right to what Adam lost.] (“the earth is the #smallcaps[Lord]’s, and the fullness thereof”); indeed, as mediator and head of the church he is heir of all. His body therefore has a right to all. On this ground the apostle says, “All things are#footnote[Heb 1:2.] yours.” So the saints, and only the saints, can with a good#footnote[Ps 24:1.] conscience use the things of this world. Those who are not of this#footnote[1 Cor 3:21.] body (whatever right and title they have before men) are only usurpers of the things they enjoy and use. They are like bankrupts who, not being worth one penny, deceitfully borrow from others, and with it keep a great table, deck and furnish their houses very lavishly, put themselves, their wives and children into fine clothes, and are merry and riotous. What is likely to be the end of such men?

8\. #emph[A right to more than Adam ever had:] namely, to Christ#footnote[8\. Saints have a right to Christ. See §. 28.] himself, and to all that belongs to him: to the purity of his nature, the perfection of his obedience, the merit of his blood, the power of his death, the virtue of his resurrection, the efficacy of his ascension. All is ours, even as the understanding, intelligence, judgment, sight, hearing, and all that is in the head belongs to the body. If the church itself were, of itself, as pure in nature, as perfect in righteousness, as powerful over death and devil and grave and hell, as able to rise from death and to ascend into heaven as Christ, it could receive no greater benefit by this than it does by these things in the person of Christ. So truly and properly are Christ himself, and all things belonging to him, the church’s. What more can be said? What more can be desired? O blessed union! Blessed are those who have a part in it!

#emph[Question.] How is it, then, that the church is so basely and miserably#footnote[Why the church is held in low esteem. 1 John 3:1.] regarded in the world?

#emph[Answer.] “The reason the world does not know us is that it did not know Him.” It does not know Christ, the head of this body; it does not know the body, which is #emph[Christ.] Let us, who know both head and body, the near union between them, and the privileges which follow from it, not be daunted either by the scoffs and scorns of the world or by our own outward weaknesses, wants, and calamities. What more would he who has Christ have?

=== §. 72. #emph[Of the privilege of our union with Christ in the time of death.]

The privilege which the saints receive by their union with Christ in the time of death (that is, all the time that passes from the departure of the saints out of this world until the general resurrection) is wonderful. For when body and soul are severed from one another, neither soul nor body is separated from Christ, but both remain united to him; even as, when Christ’s body and soul were severed from one another by death, neither his soul nor his body was separated from the Deity, but both remained united to it. This inviolable bond, which holds the saints (yes, even their very bodies as well as their souls) united to Christ in death, is the benefit of a spiritual union. If our union with Christ were bodily, it could not be so.

#emph[Objection.] Is it possible that the body which is dead should remain#footnote[Corruption of the body no hindrance to our union with Christ.] united to Christ, when it receives no power from him?

#emph[Answer.] 1. If a member of a natural body may do so, why not a member of the mystical body? That a member of a natural body may do so is evident from those who have a hand, arm, foot, leg, or any other member struck with a dead palsy. They are at times so struck that those parts receive no sense at all, nor any vigor or life from head or heart; and yet they remain true members of that body.

2\. The very dead bodies, consumed by worms or otherwise, do receive a great present benefit from their union with Christ. For by virtue of it a substance is preserved, and they are kept from destruction. Nothing is destroyed in the saints by death but that which, if it were not destroyed, would make them most miserable, namely sin. That is utterly, totally, finally destroyed in them, together with all that goes with it, which is every kind of infirmity. But the rotting of the body is only like the rotting of grain in the earth,#footnote[1 Cor 15:36.] so that it may rise a more glorious body. The metaphor of #emph[sleep,]#footnote[1 Thess 4:13.] applied to the saints when they die, shows that their bodies are not utterly destroyed.

#emph[Objection.] The bodies of all men, even of those who are not#footnote[Difference between the preservation of the saints’ bodies and others in the grave.] of this union, are preserved from utter destruction. This therefore is no benefit of our union with Christ.

#emph[Answer.] Though in the general thing itself, which is a preservation of the substance of the body, the same thing befalls the saints and the wicked, yet the means by which both are preserved, and the end for which they are preserved, are far different.

1\. The saints are preserved by a secret influence proceeding#footnote[1 Thess 4:14, 16.] from Christ as a head. In this respect they are said to have “fallen asleep in Him,” and to be “the dead in Christ.” But the wicked are reserved by an almighty power of Christ, as a terrible Lord and severe Judge.

2\. The bodies of the saints are preserved to enjoy eternal glory together with their souls; but the bodies of the wicked are reserved to be tormented in hell.

Because of these differences, the grave is like a bed to the saints,#footnote[Isa 57:2.] in which they may sleep quietly, free from all disturbance, till the day of resurrection. But it is a prison to the wicked, to hold them fast against the great day of trial, so that on Judgment Day they may be brought to appear at the bar of God’s judgment seat, and there receive the sentence of condemnation.

=== §. 73. #emph[Of the privilege of our union with Christ after death.]

The privilege which the saints receive after death by virtue of their union with Christ far surpasses all before. It may be brought under two heads.

+ Their resurrection.
+ Their glory in heaven.

What was said before of the difference between the preservation of the bodies of the saints and of the wicked in death may be applied to the difference of their resurrection.

Resurrection simply in itself is not the privilege of the saints, but “the resurrection of life”: to the wicked belongs the#footnote[John 5:29.] “resurrection of condemnation.” The benefit of resurrection arises from the glory which follows it in heaven. The apostle has excellently set forth that glory#footnote[§. 49, 50. #emph[etc.]] before, in v. 27.

=== §. 74. #emph[Of the duties required of the saints by virtue of their union with Christ.]

As the mystery of our union with Christ is a matter of great comfort and encouragement (which arises from the privileges already named), so it is also a matter of direction and incitement to us for the performing of various duties. Those who desire assurance of these privileges, and comfort by them, must be diligent and conscientious in these duties. Some of the most principal of them are these.

1\. #emph[Confidence] in Christ. Christ being our head,#footnote[Heb 3:6.] so mighty, so wise, so tender, in every way so sufficient a head as he is, we would greatly dishonor him if we did not rest ourselves wholly and only on him for every good thing, and against every evil.

2\. #emph[Subjection] answerable to his manner of governing us.#footnote[Matt 6:10. #emph[Christus habet sanctos voluntate, peccatores verò necessitate sibi subiectos.] \[Christ has the saints subject to him willingly, but sinners by necessity.\]] The world is subject to Christ by force, as he is an absolute and almighty Lord. But since he governs us as a head, we must be subject to him as members, willingly and readily. What member will rise up and rebel against the head? Indeed, what member is not as ready to obey as the head is to command?#footnote[#emph[Hier. in Eph.] 1.]

3\. A #emph[cleansing] of ourselves from all filthiness of flesh and#footnote[2 Cor 7:1.] spirit. Shall we defile the members of Christ?#footnote[1 Cor 6:15.] The sins of the saints are in this respect the more heinous, because that body, even #emph[Christ,] of which they are members, is defiled by them. Therefore, in regard of Christ the head, of the other saints their fellow members, and of themselves, all who profess themselves to be of this body must be watchful over themselves and cleanse themselves from all filthiness. Otherwise they give just occasion to think that they are no members of this body. If a lion’s foot or a bear’s paw were held out and said to be the member of a man, would anyone believe it? Can we then think that worldlings, drunkards, the profane, riotous and unclean persons, and such limbs of the devil are members of Christ?

4\. A #emph[conformity] to the image of Christ in true holiness#footnote[Eph 4:24.] and righteousness. It is not enough, therefore, for the members of Christ to abstain from polluting themselves, for they are “created in Christ Jesus to do good works.”#footnote[Eph 2:10.] “The one who remains in Me” (says Christ) “and I in him, will bear much fruit.”#footnote[John 15:5.]

5\. #emph[Heavenly affections.] “Since you#super[+] have been raised with Christ, strive for the things above, where Christ is seated at the right hand of#footnote[Col 3:1, 2.] God. Set your#super[+] affections on things above, not on earthly things.” Where our head is, there also our heart ought to be. Earthly affections do not come from that head which is in heaven, nor from that Spirit which proceeds from him. “Those who#footnote[Rom 8:5.] live according to the Spirit set their minds on the things of the Spirit.”

6\. #emph[Courage against death:] seeing that in death we are Christ’s, what cause have we to fear death? “Do not be afraid of#footnote[Luke 12:4.] those who kill the body and after that can do no more.” The ancient worthies “refused their release, so that#footnote[Heb 11:35.] they might gain a better resurrection.”

So much for the union itself. The means of bringing it about remain to be handled.

=== §. 75. #emph[Of the regeneration of those who are members of Christ.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:30.]

    #text(style: "italic")[—Of His flesh, and of His bones.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

This clause declares the means by which we come to be members of Christ, namely by receiving a new #emph[being] from Christ: to be not of the flesh and of the bones of Adam, but of the flesh and of the bones of Christ. Taken spiritually, as has been expounded#footnote[§. 70.] before, this shows that

#emph[Those who are true members of Christ’s body are truly regenerate.]#footnote[Doctrine.] “If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creature.” The apostle lays down these words#footnote[2 Cor 5:17 εἴ τις ἐν Χριστῷ, καινὴ κτίσις.] so that they serve both as a demonstration and as an exhortation #emph[(he is,] or #emph[let him be, a new creature):] neither is expressed, but either, or both, may be understood. “All of you#super[+] who were baptized into Christ”#footnote[Gal 3:27.] (that is, made members of this body) “have clothed yourselves with Christ” (that is, have been born again). The first branch points out our incorporation into Christ, the latter our regeneration.

This second man and last Adam, Christ Jesus, is “a life-giving#footnote[Reason.] spirit.”#footnote[1 Cor 15:45. Rom 8:11.] He diffuses life and grace into all his members. If his Spirit is in us, it will give life to our mortal bodies. If the head of our natural bodies conveys sense into all our members, and if the root of a tree diffuses sap into all the branches, shall not Christ much more give life to all his members?

This, then, is a test by which we may prove whether#footnote[Use.] we are indeed of this body or not, and so have a true right#footnote[Trial whether we are members of Christ.] to the privileges already named. Many boast of this honor, that they are members of Christ’s body, and yet they are not #emph[of his flesh and of his bones:] they have no other being than what they received from their parents. These vain professors are like wooden legs or arms on a man, which may be covered over with hose and sleeves for a time, but will not be raised at the resurrection with the other parts of the man’s body. So neither will those professors be raised to glory with Christ, though they may be covered over with the hose and sleeves of profession, and so seem to be members.

=== §. 76. #emph[Of Christ, the author of our regeneration.]

This relative particle (HIS), twice repeated #emph[(of] HIS #emph[flesh,]#footnote[αὐτοῦ.] #emph[and of] HIS #emph[bones),] shows that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Regeneration is from Christ.] #footnote[John 5:21.] “The Son gives life to whom He wishes.” #footnote[Doctrine.]]

#emph[Objection.] This work is attributed in Scripture to the#footnote[1 Pet 1:3.] Father, to the#footnote[John 3:5.] Spirit, to the#footnote[Jas 1:18.] word, and to the#footnote[1 Cor 4:15.] ministers of the word.

#emph[Answer.] Christ’s part may very well stand with all these. The three persons in the Trinity are all one: one in nature and essence,#footnote[#emph[Philem. v.] 10. The work of the three persons in our regeneration.] one in will and consent, one in virtue and power; what the one does, the other does also. Yet because there is a difference in their manner of working, this work (like other works) is distinctly attributed to each of them.

The Father is (if I may so speak) the beginner of this work. It was his will that his Son should be the head of a body, and that members should be made fit for that head and have a new being (“He chose to give us birth”);#footnote[Jas 1:18.] for this end he sent his Son into the world to be made flesh. The Son carried out the will of his Father: he took flesh upon him, so that we might be #emph[of his flesh.] So Christ says of himself, “I have come down from heaven … to do the will of Him who#footnote[John 6:38, 39.] sent Me. And this is the will of Him who sent Me, that I shall lose none of those He has given Me, but raise them up at the last day.” The Spirit applies to us the virtue and efficacy of the flesh of Christ, and so finishes this blessed work. “The Spirit gives life; the flesh profits nothing,”#footnote[John 6:63.] namely, of itself, without the Spirit.

So we see that ascribing this work of regeneration to Christ does not exclude the work of the Father, or of the Holy Spirit, in it, but it does exclude the work of man. It is not of ourselves, nor of our parents, nor of any other man. For we are born “not of blood, nor of the desire or will of man, but … of God”;#footnote[John 1:13.] in which respect our new birth is said to be#footnote[ἄνωθεν.] #emph[from above.]

#emph[Objection.] How then is it attributed to the word, and to the#footnote[John 3:3.] ministry of man?#footnote[How regeneration is attributed to the word, and to its ministers.]

#emph[Answer.] As to instruments which the Lord is pleased to use. Of the word it is said that #emph[God] has given us birth by the word.#footnote[Jas 1:18.] Of himself, a minister, the apostle says, “#emph[in Christ Jesus] I became your#super[+] father.”#footnote[1 Cor 4:15.] So God and Christ are joined with these instruments, or else they are not at all powerful and effectual for so great a work. For “neither he who plants nor he who waters is#footnote[1 Cor 3:7.] anything, but only God, who makes things grow.”

The work of regeneration is a new creation, a divine#footnote[Reason.] work, beyond human power. It must therefore be worked by the Lord, or it cannot be worked at all.

This is to be noted both by those who do not yet have assurance#footnote[Use.] of this blessed work done in them, and also by those who have assurance of it.

The #emph[former] may learn here where to turn for it: namely, to him who came down from heaven for that purpose, and who says, “The one who comes to Me I will never#footnote[John 6:37.] drive away.” In all the means that we use, let us look up to him and seek a blessing from him.

The #emph[latter] must, with the tenth leper, return to#footnote[Luke 17:16.] Christ and glorify God. Whatever the means were, or whoever the minister was, the praise and glory of all must be given to him.

=== §. 77. #emph[Of Christ, the matter of our regeneration.]

The preposition (OF), set down twice (OF #emph[his flesh, and]#footnote[ἐκ.] OF #emph[his bones),] being a proper sign of the material cause, shows that

#emph[Christ is not only the author, but also the matter of our new birth.] The new spiritual being which the saints have comes out of him.#footnote[Col 2:19 ἐξ οὗ πᾶν τὸ σῶμα ἐπιχορηγούμενον αὔξει τὴν αὔξησιν τοῦ Θεοῦ.] “From Him the whole body, supported …, grows as God causes it to grow.” In this respect we are said to be#footnote[Eph 1:3.] blessed with every spiritual blessing #emph[in Christ.] The metaphor of a #emph[vine,]#footnote[John 15:5.] which Christ takes to himself, also proves as much; and so do these phrases, “My flesh is real food, and My blood is real drink.”#footnote[John 6:55.]

Christ comes to be this by his incarnation. God in himself is like a bottomless and closed fountain:#footnote[Reason.] from him immediately we can receive nothing. But Christ made flesh is “a fountain opened.”#footnote[Zech 13:1.] “God was pleased to have all His fullness dwell in Him.”#footnote[Col 1:19.] “From His fullness we have all received grace upon grace.”#footnote[John 1:16.]

Behold here the benefit of Christ’s incarnation. By his taking#footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] part of our mortal flesh, we are made partakers of his spiritual flesh, namely of that spiritual life and grace which comes from him who was made flesh to convey it into us. To strengthen our faith in this the more firmly, the Lord has instituted the holy communion of his body and blood. With what conscience, reverence, and confidence ought this blessed sacrament to be celebrated?

By this doctrine we may further learn how to seek every#footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] thing from God’s hands which we desire to obtain, and how to offer that sacrifice of praise to God#footnote[Heb 13:15.] which we would have#footnote[John 16:23.] accepted: namely, in and through Jesus Christ, by whom alone we have all the communion which we have with God.#footnote[Col 3:17.] Rightly therefore does the church conclude all her forms of prayers and praises with this, or some similar clause, “through Jesus Christ our Lord.”

=== §. 78. #emph[Of the excellence of regeneration.]

The particular matter of our regeneration expressed here (the #emph[flesh] and #emph[bones] of Christ) shows that

#emph[Regeneration is a most excellent work.] Its excellence#footnote[Doctrine.] will appear the better if we compare it with the great and glorious work of our creation, and show how far it surpasses it. In this I will hold close to this metaphor, and touch#footnote[Difference between regeneration and creation.] no other differences than those it points out to us.

1\. In our creation Christ was only a #emph[worker;] but he is the very #emph[matter] of our regeneration: we are #emph[of his flesh.]

2\. The relation between Christ and man then was that of #emph[Creator] and #emph[creature;] but here it is #emph[head] and #emph[body.] “We are members of His body.”

The bond is now much nearer.

3\. The #emph[being] which we had then was from Adam; but the #emph[being] which we have now is from Christ, #emph[of] HIS #emph[flesh.]

4\. That being was only #emph[natural;] this is #emph[spiritual.] For “spirit is born#footnote[John 3:6.] of the Spirit.”

5\. Then our being was different from Christ’s; but now it is the very same as Christ’s, #emph[of his flesh.]

6\. Then man might fall clean away from the condition in which he was created (as he did), and yet Christ remain as he was. Now it cannot be so. For if any of the saints should now fall away, either Christ must fall with them, or they must be pulled from Christ, and so Christ remain a maimed body.

Behold the riches of God’s mercy. One might think it#footnote[The riches of God’s mercy.] enough, and more than man could ever have been thankful enough for, that God at first created man after his own image in a most happy condition. When we knowingly and willfully fell from it, God might justly have left us, as he did the evil angels. But he has not only restored us again to that former condition, but advanced us to a far more excellent and glorious one; in which his goodness appears to be, like his greatness, infinite and incomprehensible. Who can sufficiently set it forth? “For as high as the heavens are above the earth, so great#footnote[Ps 103:11.] is His mercy for those who fear Him.”
